# EXPECT_RUNTIME_ERROR
#~
A C++ exception thrown inside a call the JIT's bridge made ends the program
with the VM's internal-error line and exit status 1, not with a process abort.

Neither backend registers unwind information for the code it emits, so an
exception thrown in a bridge call -- by the interpreter the bridge nests for a
callee with no native code, by a trap, or by the bridge's own switch -- could
not pass through the compiled frame below it: libc++ terminated the process
(SIGABRT, exit 134, "terminating due to uncaught exception") before Execute's
catch in vm.cpp printed anything, and libstdc++ and Windows end the same way.
The bridge's two entries catch the exception now and report it as Execute
would have: the same ">>> virtual machine: internal error: ... <<<" line, the
stack, exit(1).

The regression runner passes an expected runtime error on any non-zero exit
with output, so it cannot tell the abort from the error; run_vm_flag_tests.py
(section 8) names the status and the message, and this file keeps the shape
in both regression passes. Throw is native, so it is compiled in the plain
pass too, and Runtime->SetProperty runs under the bridge either way: in the
plain pass inside the interpreter the bridge nests (a library function called
once stays interpreted), and with every method compiled through the bridge's
trap callback. Both routes end in the same catch.

The way in is Runtime->SetProperty("runtime.test.throw", ...), a key the VM
reserves so that these catches have a trigger: SetSysProp throws a
std::runtime_error carrying the value. Nothing else in the VM throws on
purpose. This file used "1e999"->ToFloat(), then a float precision property
that was not a number; both were defects, and fixing each took the trigger
away (vm_parse_ranges.obs).
~#

class JitBridgeException {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		"before"->PrintLine();
		Throw();
		"after"->PrintLine();
	}

	function : native : Throw() ~ Nil {
		Runtime->SetProperty("runtime.test.throw", "deliberate test exception");
	}
}
